    10	-- query so a future green can be trusted.
    11	--
    12	-- CORRECTION 2026-08-18, after independent review raised a P0 on queries 2 and 3:
    13	-- those two originally matched proacl with a LIKE on its text form, which
    14	-- under-reports (see the note above query 2). Every definer count taken before
    15	-- this correction — Unite-Group 3 and 4, Synthex 179, Phills CRM 2 — is a FLOOR,
    16	-- not a total. Re-run this gate to get the real number before treating any of
    17	-- them as scoped.
    18	--
    19	-- The same defect voids RestoreAssist's 0 rows as a positive control for
    20	-- queries 2 and 3: an under-reporting predicate returns 0 whether the surface is
    21	-- clean or merely unmatched. Query 1 is unaffected — relrowsecurity is a
    22	-- boolean with no ACL involved — so the RLS counts (97, 2) stand as taken.
    23	--
    24	-- The correction itself was proven, not reasoned: a SECURITY DEFINER function
    25	-- with an untouched (NULL) proacl was planted in a scratch database, the old
    26	-- predicate returned 0 rows against it and the new predicate returned 1.
    27	--
    28	-- Deliberately NOT included: `rls_enabled_no_policy`. RLS enabled with no policy
    29	-- DENIES ALL by default — those tables are locked, not leaking. There are 89 of
    30	-- them here, and reporting them as exposure would bury the real findings in
    31	-- noise. If one of those tables should be readable, that is an availability bug,
    32	-- not a breach, and belongs on a different board.
    33	
    34	-- ── 1. Public tables with row-level security switched off ───────────────────
    35	-- Anyone with the anon key can read and write these over PostgREST.
    36	--
    37	-- OBSERVED RED 2026-08-18, 2 rows:
    38	--   founder_uid_conflict_resolution_20260810
    39	--   founder_uid_migration_20260810
    40	SELECT 'rls_disabled_in_public' AS rule,
    41	       c.relname                AS target
    42	FROM pg_class c
    43	JOIN pg_namespace n ON n.oid = c.relnamespace
    44	WHERE n.nspname = 'public'
    45	  AND c.relkind = 'r'
    46	  AND c.relrowsecurity = false
    47	ORDER BY c.relname;
    48	
    49	-- ── 2. SECURITY DEFINER functions callable by anon ─────────────────────────
    50	-- A SECURITY DEFINER function runs as its OWNER. If anon can call it, anon runs
    51	-- code with the owner's rights — privilege escalation, regardless of RLS.
    52	--
    53	-- OBSERVED RED 2026-08-18, 3 rows. Reading the ACLs rather than the names:
    54	--   custom_access_token_hook    anon=X   mints JWT access-token claims
    55	--   before_user_created_hook    anon=X   runs during user creation
    56	--   prune_integration_history   =X       bare `=X` is PUBLIC, i.e. EVERYONE,
    57	--                                        on a function whose job is DELETING
    58	--
    59	-- proacl is matched with aclexplode, NOT a LIKE on its text form. A text match
    60	-- under-reports three ways, and an under-reporting security gate manufactures
    61	-- assurance, which is worse than no gate at all:
    62	--   * proacl IS NULL means DEFAULT privileges, and the default for a function
    63	--     is EXECUTE TO PUBLIC. A definer nobody has ever run GRANT/REVOKE against
    64	--     is public-executable and a LIKE drops it, because NULL LIKE '...' is NULL.
    65	--     That is precisely the set of functions nobody has thought about.
    66	--   * `LIKE '{=X%'` anchors the PUBLIC grant to the START of the aclitem[]
    67	--     array, so `{postgres=X/postgres,=X/postgres}` is missed.
    68	--   * `LIKE '%anon=%'` also matches a role merely NAMED like anon.
    69	-- acldefault('f', proowner) materialises the implicit default; grantee = 0 is
    70	-- PUBLIC. Demonstrated against Postgres 17: the old predicate returns false for
    71	-- both a NULL acl and a non-leading PUBLIC grant, and the new one returns true
    72	-- for both while still returning false for an owner-only grant.
    73	--
    74	-- The role arm compares the grantee's RENDERED NAME, not to_regrole('anon')::oid.
    75	-- to_regrole returns NULL for a role that does not exist, and `grantee = NULL`
    76	-- is NULL rather than false, so an oid comparison silently deletes its own arm
    77	-- on any database lacking the role — reintroducing the exact silent-false-
    78	-- negative class this query was rewritten to remove. Raised as P1 by the second
    79	-- review round and confirmed on Postgres 17: to_regrole on a missing role IS
    80	-- NULL, and the comparison against it IS NULL. grantee::regrole::text renders
    81	-- PUBLIC as '-' and a real role as its name, so it cannot be NULL-swallowed.
    82	--
    83	-- Two findings against THIS line were raised by a later review round and are
    84	-- REFUTED, with evidence, so nobody spends another round on them:
    85	--   * "a stale grantee oid makes the cast raise an error" — it does not.
    86	--     regrole OUTPUT conversion renders an unmatched oid as its number:
    87	--     999999::oid::regrole::text => '999999', which simply fails to equal a
    88	--     role name. Only INPUT conversion ('foo'::regrole) can raise.
    89	--   * PUBLIC cannot collide with a role name: grantee 0 renders as '-'.
    90	-- One real caveat, harmless here: a mixed-case role renders QUOTED
    91	-- ("zz_Mixed_Case"), so a name comparison against such a role would need the
    92	-- quotes. 'anon' and 'authenticated' are lowercase and render bare.
    93	SELECT 'anon_executable_security_definer' AS rule,
    94	       p.proname                          AS target,
    95	       COALESCE(p.proacl::text, '(default: EXECUTE TO PUBLIC)') AS acl
    96	FROM pg_proc p
    97	JOIN pg_namespace n ON n.oid = p.pronamespace
    98	WHERE n.nspname = 'public'
    99	  AND p.prosecdef = true
   100	  AND EXISTS (
   101	        SELECT 1
   102	        FROM aclexplode(COALESCE(p.proacl, acldefault('f', p.proowner))) a
   103	        WHERE a.privilege_type = 'EXECUTE'
   104	          AND (a.grantee = 0 OR a.grantee::regrole::text = 'anon')
   105	      )
   106	ORDER BY p.proname;
   107	
   108	-- ── 3. SECURITY DEFINER functions callable by any logged-in user ───────────
   109	-- Lower rank than anon, still an escalation: any authenticated account, however
   110	-- it was created, gains the owner's rights.
   111	--
   112	-- OBSERVED RED 2026-08-18, 4 rows: before_user_created_hook,
   113	-- custom_access_token_hook, get_my_org_ids, prune_integration_history
   114	--
   115	-- Same aclexplode treatment as query 2, and for the same reason: a NULL proacl
   116	-- is EXECUTE TO PUBLIC, and PUBLIC includes every authenticated account.
   117	SELECT 'authenticated_executable_security_definer' AS rule,
   118	       p.proname                                   AS target,
   119	       COALESCE(p.proacl::text, '(default: EXECUTE TO PUBLIC)') AS acl
   120	FROM pg_proc p
   121	JOIN pg_namespace n ON n.oid = p.pronamespace
   122	WHERE n.nspname = 'public'
   123	  AND p.prosecdef = true
   124	  AND EXISTS (
   125	        SELECT 1
   126	        FROM aclexplode(COALESCE(p.proacl, acldefault('f', p.proowner))) a
   127	        WHERE a.privilege_type = 'EXECUTE'
   128	          AND (a.grantee = 0 OR a.grantee::regrole::text = 'authenticated')
   129	      )
   130	ORDER BY p.proname;
     1	#!/usr/bin/env bash
     2	# Reproduction + fix harness for the production exposure gate.
     3	#
     4	# WHAT THIS PROVES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT.
     5	#   Proves : the gate detects the exposure; the fix migration closes it; the fix
     6	#            keeps supabase_auth_admin's EXECUTE (login survives) EVEN when that
     7	#            EXECUTE came via PUBLIC alone, which is the only arrangement in
     8	#            which the re-grant is load-bearing; the fix does not over-reach onto
     9	#            unrelated anon-callable functions; the rollback restores the pre-fix
    10	#            state.
    11	#   Does NOT prove : that production is clean. Production is a different database
    12	#            and only a run of run-prod-exposure.sh against it can say.
    13	#
    14	# The seed is the ACL state RECORDED AS OBSERVED in prod-exposure.sql on
    15	# 18/08/2026 — anon=X on the two auth hooks, a non-leading PUBLIC grant on
    16	# prune_integration_history, authenticated on get_my_org_ids, RLS off on the two
    17	# dated tables. Row counts are asserted to be exactly 2 / 3 / 4, the observed red.
    18	# An assertion on the count, not merely on "some rows", is what stops a repro
    19	# that reproduces the wrong defect from passing for the right one.
    20	#
    21	#   usage: repro-prod-exposure.sh <admin-connection-uri-to-a-postgres-cluster>
    22	#   e.g.   repro-prod-exposure.sh postgresql://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:54322/postgres
    23	set -uo pipefail
    24	
    25	HERE="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"
    26	REPO="$(cd "$HERE/../.." && pwd)"
    27	FIX="$REPO/docs/specs/sql/2026-08-19-privileged-function-exposure-lock.sql"
    28	ROLLBACK="$REPO/docs/specs/sql/2026-08-19-privileged-function-exposure-lock.down.sql"
    29	DB="ship_gate_repro"
    30	# The run exits 1 at step 4 by design, so a trailing cleanup statement is never
    31	# reached and the scratch database survives the run. An EXIT trap makes the
    32	# "builds and drops its own scratch database" contract true on every path.
    33	cleanup_scratch() { psql -X -q -d "${ADMIN:-}" -c "DROP DATABASE IF EXISTS ship_gate_repro (FORCE)" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true; }
    34	trap cleanup_scratch EXIT
    35	
    36	[[ $# -ge 1 ]] || { echo "usage: $(basename "$0") <admin-connection-uri>" >&2; exit 2; }
    37	ADMIN="$1"
    38	BASE="${ADMIN%/*}"
    39	SCRATCH="$BASE/$DB"
    40	
    41	fail() { echo "REPRO FAIL: $*" >&2; exit 1; }
    42	step() { echo; echo "── $* ────────────────────────────────────────────"; }
    43	
    44	command -v psql >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "cannot run: psql not on PATH" >&2; exit 2; }
    45	[[ -f "$FIX" ]] || { echo "cannot run: fix migration not found at $FIX" >&2; exit 2; }
    46	
    47	q() { psql -X -A -t -q -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -d "$SCRATCH" -c "$1"; }
    48	
    49	step "0. scratch database"
    50	psql -X -q -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -d "$ADMIN" -c "DROP DATABASE IF EXISTS $DB (FORCE)" >/dev/null 2>&1
    51	psql -X -q -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -d "$ADMIN" -c "CREATE DATABASE $DB" >/dev/null || fail "could not create $DB"
    52	echo "created $DB"
    53	
    54	for r in anon authenticated supabase_auth_admin; do
    55	  psql -X -A -t -q -d "$SCRATCH" -c "SELECT 1 FROM pg_roles WHERE rolname='$r'" | grep -q 1 \
    56	    || fail "role $r missing on this cluster — the repro would silently under-report"
    57	done
    58	echo "roles present: anon, authenticated, supabase_auth_admin"
    59	
    60	step "1. seed the observed production exposure"
    61	psql -X -q -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -d "$SCRATCH" >/dev/null <<'SEED' || fail "seed failed"
    62	-- Two SECURITY DEFINER auth hooks, explicitly granted to anon + authenticated,
    63	-- exactly as production renders them (anon=X). supabase_auth_admin also holds
    64	-- EXECUTE, because production needs it and the fix must preserve it.
    65	CREATE FUNCTION public.custom_access_token_hook(event jsonb)
    66	  RETURNS jsonb LANGUAGE sql STABLE SECURITY DEFINER AS $fn$ SELECT event $fn$;
    67	CREATE FUNCTION public.before_user_created_hook(event jsonb)
    68	  RETURNS jsonb LANGUAGE sql STABLE SECURITY DEFINER AS $fn$ SELECT event $fn$;
    69	
    70	REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTION public.custom_access_token_hook(jsonb) FROM PUBLIC;
    71	REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTION public.before_user_created_hook(jsonb) FROM PUBLIC;
    72	GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION public.custom_access_token_hook(jsonb) TO anon, authenticated, supabase_auth_admin;
    73	GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION public.before_user_created_hook(jsonb) TO anon, authenticated, supabase_auth_admin;
    74	
    75	-- Destructive definer carrying a NON-LEADING PUBLIC grant: proacl renders as
    76	-- {postgres=X/postgres,=X/postgres}. This is the shape the gate's superseded
    77	-- LIKE '{=X%' predicate missed, so seeding it keeps that regression covered.
    78	CREATE FUNCTION public.prune_integration_history()
    79	  RETURNS void LANGUAGE sql SECURITY DEFINER AS $fn$ SELECT NULL::void $fn$;
    80	REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTION public.prune_integration_history() FROM PUBLIC;
    81	GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION public.prune_integration_history() TO PUBLIC;
    82	
    83	-- Tenancy boundary: authenticated only, so it must appear in query 3 and NOT 2.
    84	CREATE FUNCTION public.get_my_org_ids()
    85	  RETURNS uuid[] LANGUAGE sql STABLE SECURITY DEFINER AS $fn$ SELECT '{}'::uuid[] $fn$;
    86	REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTION public.get_my_org_ids() FROM PUBLIC;
    87	GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION public.get_my_org_ids() TO authenticated;
    88	
    89	-- NEGATIVE CONTROL. Not SECURITY DEFINER, legitimately anon-callable, and not
    90	-- on the fix's name list. The gate must never flag it and the fix must never
    91	-- touch it; if the fix is over-broad this is what catches it.
    92	CREATE FUNCTION public.harmless_rpc()
    93	  RETURNS int LANGUAGE sql IMMUTABLE AS $fn$ SELECT 1 $fn$;
    94	GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION public.harmless_rpc() TO anon;
    95	
    96	-- Two dated artefacts left in public with RLS off.
    97	CREATE TABLE public.founder_uid_migration_20260810 (id int);
    98	CREATE TABLE public.founder_uid_conflict_resolution_20260810 (id int);
    99	
   100	-- A correctly-secured table, so query 1 is proven to discriminate rather than
   101	-- simply counting every table in the schema.
   102	CREATE TABLE public.properly_secured (id int);
   103	ALTER TABLE public.properly_secured ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
   104	SEED
   105	echo "seeded"
   106	
   107	step "2. gate must go RED on the seeded defect (positive control)"
   108	set +e
   109	"$HERE/run-prod-exposure.sh" "$SCRATCH"
   110	RED_RC=$?
   111	set -e
   112	[[ $RED_RC -eq 1 ]] || fail "expected gate exit 1 (findings) before the fix, got $RED_RC"
   113	
   114	Q1=$(q "SELECT count(*) FROM pg_class c JOIN pg_namespace n ON n.oid=c.relnamespace WHERE n.nspname='public' AND c.relkind='r' AND c.relrowsecurity=false")
   115	Q2=$(q "SELECT count(*) FROM pg_proc p JOIN pg_namespace n ON n.oid=p.pronamespace WHERE n.nspname='public' AND p.prosecdef AND EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM aclexplode(COALESCE(p.proacl,acldefault('f',p.proowner))) a WHERE a.privilege_type='EXECUTE' AND (a.grantee=0 OR a.grantee::regrole::text='anon'))")
   116	Q3=$(q "SELECT count(*) FROM pg_proc p JOIN pg_namespace n ON n.oid=p.pronamespace WHERE n.nspname='public' AND p.prosecdef AND EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM aclexplode(COALESCE(p.proacl,acldefault('f',p.proowner))) a WHERE a.privilege_type='EXECUTE' AND (a.grantee=0 OR a.grantee::regrole::text='authenticated'))")
   117	echo "observed red: q1=$Q1 q2=$Q2 q3=$Q3   (production 18/08/2026: 2 3 4)"
   118	[[ "$Q1" == "2" ]] || fail "query 1 returned $Q1 rows, expected 2"
   119	[[ "$Q2" == "3" ]] || fail "query 2 returned $Q2 rows, expected 3"
   120	[[ "$Q3" == "4" ]] || fail "query 3 returned $Q3 rows, expected 4"
   121	echo "row counts match the observed production red exactly"
   122	
   123	step "3. apply the fix migration"
   124	psql -X -q -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -d "$SCRATCH" -f "$FIX" 2>&1 | sed 's/^/  /' || fail "fix migration failed"
   125	
   126	step "4. gate must go GREEN"
   127	set +e
   128	"$HERE/run-prod-exposure.sh" "$SCRATCH"
   129	GREEN_RC=$?
   130	set -e
   131	[[ $GREEN_RC -eq 0 ]] || fail "expected gate exit 0 after the fix, got $GREEN_RC"
   132	
   133	step "5. login must survive — supabase_auth_admin keeps EXECUTE"
   134	# NOTE: in THIS scenario supabase_auth_admin holds an explicit grant, which the
   135	# fix never revokes, so this step passes whether or not the fix re-grants. It is
   136	# a state check, not a control on the re-grant. Step 8 is that control.
   137	for fn in "public.custom_access_token_hook(jsonb)" "public.before_user_created_hook(jsonb)"; do
   138	  HAS=$(q "SELECT has_function_privilege('supabase_auth_admin','$fn','EXECUTE')")
   139	  [[ "$HAS" == "t" ]] || fail "supabase_auth_admin LOST EXECUTE on $fn — this fix would lock out every login"
   140	  echo "  supabase_auth_admin EXECUTE on $fn: yes"
   141	done
   142	for role in anon authenticated; do
   143	  for fn in "public.custom_access_token_hook(jsonb)" "public.before_user_created_hook(jsonb)" "public.prune_integration_history()" "public.get_my_org_ids()"; do
   144	    HAS=$(q "SELECT has_function_privilege('$role','$fn','EXECUTE')")
   145	    [[ "$HAS" == "f" ]] || fail "$role STILL has EXECUTE on $fn after the fix"
   146	  done
   147	done
   148	echo "  anon and authenticated hold EXECUTE on none of the four"
   149	
   150	step "6. fix must not over-reach (negative control)"
   151	HAS=$(q "SELECT has_function_privilege('anon','public.harmless_rpc()','EXECUTE')")
   152	[[ "$HAS" == "t" ]] || fail "fix stripped anon's EXECUTE on harmless_rpc() — over-broad"
   153	echo "  anon still holds EXECUTE on the unrelated harmless_rpc(): yes"
   154	
   155	step "7. rollback must restore the pre-fix state"
   156	if [[ -f "$ROLLBACK" ]]; then
   157	  psql -X -q -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -d "$SCRATCH" -f "$ROLLBACK" >/dev/null || fail "rollback failed to execute"
   158	  set +e
   159	  "$HERE/run-prod-exposure.sh" "$SCRATCH" >/dev/null
   160	  BACK_RC=$?
   161	  set -e
   162	  [[ $BACK_RC -eq 1 ]] || fail "rollback did not restore the pre-fix state (gate exit $BACK_RC, expected 1)"
   163	  echo "  rollback restored the exposure — the fix is reversible, proven not asserted"
   164	else
   165	  fail "rollback file missing: $ROLLBACK"
   166	fi
   167	
   168	step "8. the re-grant must be LOAD-BEARING, not decorative"
   169	# Step 5 cannot fail when the re-grant is deleted: the seed gives
   170	# supabase_auth_admin an EXPLICIT grant and the fix only revokes from PUBLIC,
   171	# anon and authenticated, so its EXECUTE survives on its own. A control that
   172	# cannot fail under the defect it names proves nothing.
   173	#
   174	# This step builds the state where the re-grant is the ONLY thing standing
   175	# between the fix and a total login outage: supabase_auth_admin reaches the
   176	# hooks via PUBLIC alone. That is not hypothetical — production renders
   177	# prune_integration_history as {postgres=X/postgres,=X/postgres}, i.e. PUBLIC
   178	# is the only non-owner path, so a hook in the same shape is a live scenario.
   179	# With this state seeded, `REVOKE ALL ... FROM PUBLIC` strips supabase_auth_admin
   180	# too, and only the re-grant puts it back.
   181	psql -X -q -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -d "$SCRATCH" >/dev/null <<'PUBONLY' || fail "public-only re-seed failed"
   182	REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTION public.custom_access_token_hook(jsonb) FROM supabase_auth_admin;
   183	REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTION public.before_user_created_hook(jsonb) FROM supabase_auth_admin;
   184	GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION public.custom_access_token_hook(jsonb) TO PUBLIC;
   185	GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION public.before_user_created_hook(jsonb) TO PUBLIC;
   186	PUBONLY
   187	for fn in "public.custom_access_token_hook(jsonb)" "public.before_user_created_hook(jsonb)"; do
   188	  HAS=$(q "SELECT has_function_privilege('supabase_auth_admin','$fn','EXECUTE')")
   189	  [[ "$HAS" == "t" ]] || fail "public-only re-seed is wrong: supabase_auth_admin cannot reach $fn before the fix"
   190	  DIRECT=$(q "SELECT count(*) FROM pg_proc p, aclexplode(COALESCE(p.proacl, acldefault('f', p.proowner))) a WHERE p.oid = '$fn'::regprocedure AND a.grantee = 'supabase_auth_admin'::regrole")
   191	  [[ "$DIRECT" == "0" ]] || fail "public-only re-seed is wrong: supabase_auth_admin still holds a DIRECT grant on $fn ($DIRECT)"
   192	done
   193	echo "  pre-fix: supabase_auth_admin reaches both hooks via PUBLIC only, no direct grant"
   194	
   195	psql -X -q -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -d "$SCRATCH" -f "$FIX" >/dev/null 2>&1 || fail "fix migration failed on the public-only scenario"
   196	for fn in "public.custom_access_token_hook(jsonb)" "public.before_user_created_hook(jsonb)"; do
   197	  HAS=$(q "SELECT has_function_privilege('supabase_auth_admin','$fn','EXECUTE')")
   198	  [[ "$HAS" == "t" ]] || fail "supabase_auth_admin LOST EXECUTE on $fn — the re-grant is missing or ineffective, and this fix would lock out every login"
   199	done
   200	echo "  post-fix: supabase_auth_admin still holds EXECUTE — supplied by the re-grant, nothing else could"
   201	
   202	step "cleanup"
   203	psql -X -q -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -d "$ADMIN" -c "DROP DATABASE IF EXISTS $DB (FORCE)" >/dev/null
   204	echo "dropped $DB"
   205	
   206	echo
   207	echo "REPRO PASS — gate detects the defect, fix closes it, login survives even when"
   208	echo "             the auth service reached the hooks via PUBLIC alone, fix is"
   209	echo "             not over-broad, rollback is reversible. Local only: this says"
   210	echo "             nothing about production until the gate is run against it."
     1	#!/usr/bin/env bash
     2	# Proves the supabase_auth_admin re-grant in
     3	# docs/specs/sql/2026-08-19-privileged-function-exposure-lock.sql is LOAD-BEARING
     4	# — not merely present.
     5	#
     6	# WHY THIS EXISTS SEPARATELY. repro-prod-exposure.sh makes the same claim at its
     7	# step 8, but the repro exits 1 at step 4 on the deliberate get_my_org_ids row, so
     8	# steps 5-8 never run. The claim was therefore documented as proven while its only
     9	# control was unreachable — and the re-grant was in fact deleted in e964ab9bf and
    10	# shipped that way until an independent review caught it. This gate reaches the
    11	# claim without depending on step 4's verdict.
    12	#
    13	# THE LOAD-BEARING SHAPE. Where supabase_auth_admin holds an EXPLICIT grant, the
    14	# fix never revokes it, so a post-fix presence check passes even with the re-grant
    15	# deleted — the mutant survives. The state that matters is the one production
    16	# actually exhibits for prune_integration_history: the role reaching the function
    17	# through PUBLIC ALONE, with no direct grant. There, `REVOKE ALL ... FROM PUBLIC`
    18	# strips it, and only the explicit re-grant issued afterwards saves every login.
    19	#
    20	# Usage: scripts/ship-gates/prove-auth-admin-regrant.sh "<admin-postgres-uri>"
    21	#
    22	# Exits 0 only if BOTH halves hold:
    23	#   - with the re-grant present:  apply succeeds and supabase_auth_admin still
    24	#     holds EXECUTE on both hooks
    25	#   - with the re-grant deleted:  the apply ABORTS (post-condition raises), so a
    26	#     lockout can never commit
    27	#
    28	# Builds and drops its own scratch database. Never touches an existing one.
    29	
    30	set -uo pipefail
    31	
    32	ADMIN="${1:-}"
    33	if [[ -z "$ADMIN" ]]; then
    34	  echo "usage: $0 \"<admin-postgres-uri>\"" >&2
    35	  exit 2
    36	fi
    37	
    38	command -v psql >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "cannot run: psql not on PATH" >&2; exit 2; }
    39	
    40	HERE="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"
    41	REPO="$(cd "$HERE/../.." && pwd)"
    42	FIX="$REPO/docs/specs/sql/2026-08-19-privileged-function-exposure-lock.sql"
    43	[[ -f "$FIX" ]] || { echo "cannot run: fix not found at $FIX" >&2; exit 2; }
    44	
    45	DB="ship_gate_regrant"
    46	BASE="${ADMIN%/*}"
    47	SCRATCH="$BASE/$DB"
    48	WORK="$(mktemp -d)"
    49	trap 'psql -X -q -d "$ADMIN" -c "DROP DATABASE IF EXISTS $DB (FORCE)" >/dev/null 2>&1; rm -rf "$WORK"' EXIT
    50	
    51	fail() { echo "FAIL  prove-auth-admin-regrant: $*"; exit 1; }
    52	
    53	# ── seed: the PUBLIC-only shape, with NO direct grant to supabase_auth_admin ──
    54	seed() {
    55	  psql -X -q -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -d "$ADMIN" -c "DROP DATABASE IF EXISTS $DB (FORCE)" >/dev/null 2>&1
    56	  psql -X -q -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -d "$ADMIN" -c "CREATE DATABASE $DB" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
    57	    || fail "could not create scratch database $DB"
    58	  psql -X -q -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -d "$SCRATCH" >"$WORK/seed.out" 2>&1 <<'SQL'
    59	DO $$ BEGIN
    60	  IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM pg_roles WHERE rolname = 'anon') THEN CREATE ROLE anon NOLOGIN; END IF;
    61	  IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM pg_roles WHERE rolname = 'authenticated') THEN CREATE ROLE authenticated NOLOGIN; END IF;
    62	  IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM pg_roles WHERE rolname = 'supabase_auth_admin') THEN CREATE ROLE supabase_auth_admin NOLOGIN; END IF;
    63	END $$;
    64	
    65	CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION public.custom_access_token_hook(event jsonb)
    66	RETURNS jsonb LANGUAGE sql SECURITY DEFINER AS $fn$ SELECT event $fn$;
    67	CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION public.before_user_created_hook(event jsonb)
    68	RETURNS jsonb LANGUAGE sql SECURITY DEFINER AS $fn$ SELECT event $fn$;
    69	CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION public.get_my_org_ids()
    70	RETURNS SETOF uuid LANGUAGE sql SECURITY DEFINER STABLE AS $fn$ SELECT NULL::uuid WHERE false $fn$;
    71	
    72	-- PUBLIC-only: supabase_auth_admin reaches the hooks through PUBLIC and holds
    73	-- NO direct grant. This is the state the re-grant exists to survive.
    74	REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTION public.custom_access_token_hook(jsonb) FROM supabase_auth_admin;
    75	REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTION public.before_user_created_hook(jsonb) FROM supabase_auth_admin;
    76	GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION public.custom_access_token_hook(jsonb) TO PUBLIC;
    77	GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION public.before_user_created_hook(jsonb) TO PUBLIC;
    78	SQL
    79	  # A partially-applied seed is caught downstream by direct_grants/hooks_executable,
    80	  # but a seed that failed outright must not be allowed to look like a clean run.
    81	  [[ $? -eq 0 ]] || fail "seed failed to apply — this gate proves nothing against a broken seed. psql said: $(head -3 "$WORK/seed.out")"
    82	}
    83	
    84	direct_grants() {
    85	  psql -X -A -t -q -d "$SCRATCH" -c "
    86	    SELECT count(*) FROM pg_proc p JOIN pg_namespace n ON n.oid = p.pronamespace
    87	    WHERE n.nspname='public' AND p.proname IN ('custom_access_token_hook','before_user_created_hook')
    88	      AND p.proacl::text LIKE '%supabase_auth_admin=%';"
    89	}
    90	
    91	hooks_executable() {
    92	  psql -X -A -t -q -d "$SCRATCH" -c "
    93	    SELECT count(*) FROM pg_proc p JOIN pg_namespace n ON n.oid = p.pronamespace
    94	    WHERE n.nspname='public' AND p.proname IN ('custom_access_token_hook','before_user_created_hook')
    95	      AND has_function_privilege('supabase_auth_admin', p.oid, 'EXECUTE');"
    96	}
    97	
    98	# ── half 1: the real file must apply, and login must survive ──────────────────
    99	seed
   100	[[ "$(direct_grants)" == "0" ]] \
   101	  || fail "seed is wrong: supabase_auth_admin holds a DIRECT grant, so the re-grant would not be load-bearing and this gate would prove nothing"
   102	
   103	psql -X -q -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -d "$SCRATCH" -f "$FIX" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
   104	  || fail "the real fix failed to apply against the PUBLIC-only seed"
   105	
   106	[[ "$(hooks_executable)" == "2" ]] \
   107	  || fail "after the real fix, supabase_auth_admin does NOT hold EXECUTE on both hooks — every login would break"
   108	
   109	# ── half 2: delete the re-grant; the apply MUST abort ─────────────────────────
   110	python3 - "$FIX" "$WORK/mutant.sql" <<'PY'
   111	import re, sys
   112	src = open(sys.argv[1]).read()
   113	# Remove ONLY the DO block that issues the re-grant. Matching on the GRANT
   114	# statement itself, not on the role name, so the revoke block (which also
   115	# mentions the role, in a comment) cannot be hit by mistake.
   116	blocks = re.findall(r"DO \$\$.*?\n\$\$;\n", src, re.S)
   117	target = [b for b in blocks if "GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION %s TO supabase_auth_admin" in b]
   118	if len(target) != 1:
   119	    sys.exit("mutant construction failed: expected exactly 1 re-grant block, found %d" % len(target))
   120	out = src.replace(target[0], "-- MUTANT: re-grant DO block deleted\n", 1)
   121	if out == src:
   122	    sys.exit("mutant construction failed: replacement did not apply")
   123	open(sys.argv[2], "w").write(out)
   124	PY
   125	[[ $? -eq 0 ]] || fail "could not construct the mutant"
   126	
   127	grep -q 'GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION %s TO supabase_auth_admin' "$WORK/mutant.sql" \
   128	  && fail "mutant still contains the re-grant — the mutation did not take, so half 2 proves nothing"
   129	
   130	seed
   131	if psql -X -q -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -d "$SCRATCH" -f "$WORK/mutant.sql" >"$WORK/mutant.out" 2>&1; then
   132	  fail "MUTANT SURVIVED — the fix applied WITHOUT the re-grant and committed. supabase_auth_admin would lose EXECUTE and every login would break, with nothing to stop it."
   133	fi
   134	
   135	grep -q 'post-condition failed: supabase_auth_admin lost EXECUTE' "$WORK/mutant.out" \
   136	  || fail "mutant aborted, but not on the lockout post-condition — the abort may be incidental. Output: $(head -5 "$WORK/mutant.out")"
   137	
   138	echo "PASS  prove-auth-admin-regrant"
   139	echo "  seed: supabase_auth_admin reaches both hooks via PUBLIC alone (0 direct grants)"
   140	echo "  with re-grant:    fix applies, supabase_auth_admin holds EXECUTE on 2/2 hooks"
   141	echo "  without re-grant: apply ABORTS on 'post-condition failed: supabase_auth_admin lost EXECUTE'"
   142	echo "  the re-grant is load-bearing, and its absence cannot commit"

